Ah, so you're claiming to be part of the mind-reader crowd, are you?
You can go sit in the corner with Jove, another imbecile who believes
that I'm approaching this from the personal goal of "spoil others' fun"
rather than the perspective of "the game should have no obvious design
flaws, and thus be as good/balanced as it possibly can."  (rolls eyes)
Please, make an effort to use your head.

Let me explain something to you.  Nethack is 20 years old.  I would
enjoy seeing Nethack become 40 years old and equally or more popular
than it is today.  To do that though, we're going to need new players
coming as older ones drift away.

On #nethack, we see a regular stream of new players come in, referred by
others who found the game, know the game, or whatever.  These new
players are often a bit younger than those of us who've been around for
awhile, and as such they're used to games with modern graphics, flashing
lights, ... Oh, and also, games which DON'T have glaring "you mean all I
have to do is repeat this one action over and over and i get good enough
to win?" flaws in them.

Nethack is already at a disadvantage to these other games in their minds
because it will NEVER have modern graphics.  Fortunately, it makes up
for it in gameplay... and thus, that gameplay must be of a superior
design.  Major flaws and imbalances like pudding (or whatever) farming
do not help us convince those new players that the gameplay is
"superior" in some fashion; instead, they detract from it in a big way.

And thus, a new player watches someone else pudding farming, and whether
he does it himself or not, immediately puts the game in the class of
"beat once, forget forever", rather than "explore all the nooks and
crannies of."  By the way, we've HEARD exactly this from new players;
this isn't open to debate.  We had one new player ascend normally once,
pudding farm and ascend to gain what was then spot #3 on the top 2000
list, play a couple more games, and then vanish completely.  Boy, that's
just what I want new players to do -- focus on "beat the game and
leave", rather than hang out and become part of the community.

Do you understand now?

